0.34.012.875 D srv  log_server_r: request:  {"model": "unsloth/Qwen3.8-27B-GGUF", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "{\"instruction\":\"For each requested answer sentence emit index|E/C/B|p_unsupported with two decimals|[zero to two source segment indices]. One record per line in input order; no other text. Relations and addresses are predictions.\",\"source\":\"{\\\"passages\\\":\\\"passage 1:The two-toed sloth is slightly bigger than the three-toed sloth, though they share many of the same features. They are about the size of a medium-sized dog at around 23 to 27 inches (58 to 68 cm) and 17.5 to 18.75 pounds (about 8 kilograms).he two-toed sloth is slightly bigger than the three-toed sloth, though they share many of the same features. They are about the size of a medium-sized dog at around 23 to 27 inches (58 to 68 cm) and 17.5 to 18.75 pounds (about 8 kilograms).\\\\n\\\\npassage 2:1 Two-toed and three-toed sloths were formerly placed in the same family but the two genera have profound behavioral and anatomical differences and are believed to come from two different fossil lineages. 2  They are now placed in separate families. 3  2-toed is larger, faster, and nocturnal. Long limbs \u2014 forelegs only slightly longer than hind legs (3-toed sloth's forelegs are 35% longer than hind legs). 2  Two-toed sloths have hands with two functioning fingers with sharp claws 76 - 102 mm (3 - 4 inches). 3  Three-toed sloths have three fingers on their hands. 4  Three claws on hind limbs.\\\\n\\\\npassage 3:1 Besides the fact that one has two toes on its forelimbs and the other three, they have different numbers of vertebrae (three-toed ones have nine; two-toed ones have six or seven). 2  Also, Three-toed sloths have a small tail and its forelegs are substantially longer than the rear ones.ifference Between Two and Three Toed Sloths: 1  Besides the fact that one has two toes on its forelimbs and the other three, they have different numbers of vertebrae (three-toed ones have nine; two-toed ones have six or seven).\\\\n\\\\n\\\",\\\"question\\\":\\\"what makes the two toed sloth different\\\"}\",\"answer\":\"Based on the provided passages, the main differences between the two-toed sloth (Choloepus didactylus) and the three-toed sloth (Bradypus variegatus) are:\\n\\n1. Number of toes on the front limbs: The two-toed sloth has two toes on each front limb, while the three-toed sloth has three toes.\\n2. Size: The two-toed sloth is generally smaller than the three-toed sloth, with a length of around 23-27 inches (58-68 cm) and a weight of around 17.5-18.75 pounds (about 8 kilograms), compared to the three-toed sloth's length of around 30-40 inches (76-102 cm) and weight of around 20-30 pounds (around 9-14 kilograms).\\n3. Limb structure: The two-toed sloth has longer limbs overall, with the forelegs being slightly longer than the hind legs, while the three-toed sloth has shorter forelegs that are 35% longer than the hind legs.\\n4. Hand structure: The two-toed sloth has hands with two functioning fingers and sharp claws, while the three-toed sloth has three fingers on its hands.\\n5. Vertebral count: The two-toed sloth has six or seven vertebrae in its body, while the three-toed sloth has nine vertebrae.\\n6. Tail length: The three-toed sloth has a smaller tail than the two-toed sloth.\\n7. Forelimb length: The three-toed sloth has substantially longer forelegs than the rear legs, while the two-toed sloth has more balanced proportions.\",\"source_segments\":[{\"index\":0,\"byte_start\":0,\"byte_end\":133},{\"index\":1,\"byte_start\":133,\"byte_end\":373},{\"index\":2,\"byte_start\":373,\"byte_end\":723},{\"index\":3,\"byte_start\":723,\"byte_end\":768},{\"index\":4,\"byte_start\":768,\"byte_end\":812},{\"index\":5,\"byte_start\":812,\"byte_end\":929},{\"index\":6,\"byte_start\":929,\"byte_end\":1033},{\"index\":7,\"byte_start\":1033,\"byte_end\":1089},{\"index\":8,\"byte_start\":1089,\"byte_end\":1314},{\"index\":9,\"byte_start\":1314,\"