the independent South Carolina Law Enforcement Division. Gann said when he arrived at the shooting scene, an officer identified only as Habersham \\\"was administering first aid\\\" to Scott. \\\"I exited my vehicle and assisted Officer Habersham with first aid and CPR to the driver,\\\" Gann said in the report. \\\"We continued to perform first aid and CPR until EMS arrived... When EMS and first responders arrived, EMS took care over providing care to the driver, who was pronounced deceased a short time later.\\\" Habersham, in his account, did not mention performing CPR. \\\"I attempted to render aid to the victim by applying pressure to the gunshot wounds and (directing) the best route for EMS and fire to get to the victim faster,\\\" he said in the report. An officer identified as Sgt. Webb said that he requested an ambulance. Webb said that at 9:41 a.m. he saw Habersham \\\"administering chest compression to the defendant.\\\" North Charleston Police Chief  Eddie Driggers was asked at a news conference this week whether CPR was performed on Scott. \\\"I do not know. I was told that life-saving ... that they tried to save his life,\\\" Driggers said. North Charleston Mayor Keith Summey added that not every North Charleston police officer is CPR certified. What we know about Officer Slager.\\n\",\"answer\":\"South Carolina police officer Michael Slager has been charged with murder after shooting an unarmed black man, Walter Scott, eight times. A video of the incident, taken by passerby Feidin Santana, shows that Slager shot at Scott as he ran away, contrary to the initial police report which stated that Scott had tried to take Slager's Taser. The video shows a dark object falling behind Scott and hitting the ground before Slager opens fire, but it is unclear whether this is the Taser. Santana says that although he heard the sound of a Taser being used, he did not see Scott grab it. Slager was the first officer on the scene, and reports from other officers suggest that they arrived after the shooting had taken place. The police report does not include a statement from Slager. As the South Carolina Law Enforcement Division is currently investigating the incident, the North Charleston Police Department is not providing any further information. Slager has been fired from the police force.\",\"source_segments\":[{\"index\":0,\"byte_start\":0,\"byte_end\":194},{\"index\":1,\"byte_start\":194,\"byte_end\":282},{\"index\":2,\"byte_start\":282,\"byte_end\":477},{\"index\":3,\"byte_start\":477,\"byte_end\":503},{\"index\":4,\"byte_start\":503,\"byte_end\":581},{\"index\":5,\"byte_start\":581,\"byte_end\":641},{\"index\":6,\"byte_start\":641,\"byte_end\":743},{\"index\":7,\"byte_start\":743,\"byte_end\":844},{\"index\":8,\"byte_start\":844,\"byte_end\":884},{\"index\":9,\"byte_start\":884,\"byte_end\":990},{\"index\":10,\"byte_start\":990,\"byte_end\":1056},{\"index\":11,\"byte_start\":1056,\"byte_end\":1109},{\"index\":12,\"byte_start\":1109,\"byte_end\":1207},{\"index\":13,\"byte_start\":1207,\"byte_end\":1261},{\"index\":14,\"byte_start\":1261,\"byte_end\":1454},{\"index\":15,\"byte_start\":1454,\"byte_end\":1548},{\"index\":16,\"byte_start\":1548,\"byte_end\":1584},{\"index\":17,\"byte_start\":1584,\"byte_end\":1627},{\"index\":18,\"byte_start\":1627,\"byte_end\":1690},{\"index\":19,\"byte_start\":1690,\"byte_end\":1716},{\"index\":20,\"byte_start\":1716,\"byte_end\":1771},{\"index\":21,\"byte_start\":1771,\"byte_end\":1821},{\"index\":22,\"byte_start\":1821,\"byte_end\":1896},{\"index\":23,\"byte_start\":1896,\"byte_end\":1918},{\"index\":24,\"byte_start\":1918,\"byte_end\":2021},{\"index\":25,\"byte_start\":2021,\"byte_end\":2053},{\"index\":26,\"byte_start\":2053,\"byte_end\":2139},{\"index\":27,\"byte_start\":2139,\"byte_end\":2246},{\"index\":28,\"byte_start\":2246,\"byte_end\":2377},{\"index\":29,\"byte_start\":2377,\"byte_end\":2480},{\"index\":30,\"byte_start\":2480,\"byte_end\":2499},{\"index\":31,\"byte_start\":2499,\"by