d on Muslims, all because of their faith. The mosque raised money, collected food, blankets, medicine. Promises were made to provide more, and regularly. But we all knew that wasn't enough. As we left the mosque, my peers and I were disgruntled and confused. Shocked. Angry. Our teachers could've told us: Go and fight. Defend your Muslim brothers and sisters who are under siege. Or they could've told us to keep our heads down and make money and live comfortably. Neither answer would have satisfied. Fortunately for us, they offered us a third way. They showed us, patiently, how to work with others, how to compromise, how to get things done. A more engaged American Muslim community, they explained, could use its resources to help people suffering all around the world. They were right. We saw the dead-end road of radicalism from afar, but we also saw, up close, how communities that isolated themselves and turned inward found themselves powerless, ineffectual and ignored. Thanks to social media, a medium that the world's burgeoning young Muslim population is increasingly comfortable with, more Muslims can and will see this, too. Radicalism will be done in by fellow Muslims who want to save their religion from this monster within it.  It's happening already. Our national conversation about Islam is focused on the wrong issues. Does Islam need a Reformation? What in Islam causes violence?  We would do a lot better if we accepted that Muslims the world over have real grievances \u2014 dictatorships, corruption, foreign intervention, religious illiteracy, lack of economic opportunity -- and radicals exploit these. We need to show the young Dzhokhars that, if they want to help, then violence isn't going to help. To fight extremism, we need to pose this question to young Muslims: \\\"Do you want to help your brothers and sisters in faith?\\\"   Because those who claim to be defending us are making things so much worse. Their narrative has failed. Their solution is bankrupt. The Caliph wears no clothes. It's the reason why increasing numbers of Muslims reject extremism -- and not just because our numbers are increasing.\\n\",\"answer\":\"After two days of deliberation, the jury found Dzhokhar Tsarnaev guilty of all charges related to the Boston Marathon bombing. While the verdict may not come as a surprise, the question remains of how to prevent similar acts of violence in the future. One issue is the growing number of young Muslims, with nearly one-third of the global population expected to be Muslim by 2050. However, it is important to recognize that Islam does not inherently promote extremism, and that the actions of a small minority of radicals should not be used to stereotype and stigmatize an entire religious community. Instead, understanding the root causes of radicalization is key, including political, social, and economic factors.\\n\\nThe author argues that the solution to fighting extremism is not to reform Islam, but rather to show young Muslims that violence is not the answer. This can be achieved by highlighting the positive impact that Muslims can have on the world through community engagement, compromise, and working together to address common issues. By posing the question to young Muslims of whether they want to help their brothers and sisters in faith, it is possible to counter the narrative of extremist groups and promote a more peaceful and constructive approach to addressing global problems. Ultimately, it is important to recognize that the battle against extremism is not a struggle between religions or cultures, but rather a struggle for the hearts and minds of individuals who are seeking a sense of purpose and meaning in a complex and often chaotic world.\",\"source_segments\":[{\"index\":0,\"byte_start\":0,\"byte_end\":116},{\"index\":1,\"byte_start\":116,\"byte_end\":146},{\"index\":2,\"byte_start\":146,\"byte_end\":246},{\"index\":3,\"byte_start\":246,\"byte_end\":410},{\"index\":4,\"byte_start\":410,\"byte_end\