tomers on request,\\\" the State Water Resources Control Board declared in March under expanded emergency regulations. Tourists can also expect to hear a lot of requests at hotels about whether they want their linens and towels laundered daily. These requests are mandatory under the new regulations. And they'll see fewer homes running decorative fountains. Because much of the snowpack in the Sierra Nevada has alarmingly disappeared, many ski resorts shut down early this year, including at Lake Tahoe, and some are now building zip lines, mountain bike trails and wedding venues to keep tourists coming, the Sacramento Bee reported. \\\"If the drought continues through next winter and we do not conserve more, the consequences could be even more catastrophic than they already are,\\\" State Water Board Chair Felicia Marcus said in March. But what about those yummy California wines, you ask? Guess what. They're only getting better -- because of the drought. Yes, you read that right. The 2014 wine grape harvest was \\\"third in a string of great vintages this decade,\\\" the Wine Institute says. \\\"California vintners and growers across the state are grateful for another excellent vintage, despite an ongoing drought and earthquake that rocked south Napa in late August just as crush was getting underway,\\\" the institute said in a statement last year. \\\"A mild winter and spring caused early bud break, although the overall length of the growing season was similar to past years.\\\" Wine grapes use relatively low water, said institute spokeswoman Gladys Horiuchi. \\\"Yes, drought years tend to produce terrific quality,\\\" she added. \\\"With the record high California wine grape harvests in 2012, 2013 and 2014, there is a good supply of California wine.\\\" That may be the only thing to toast about this drought.\\n\",\"answer\":\"The ongoing drought in California is not only affecting the state itself but also the rest of the country. California is a major producer of fruits and vegetables, accounting for over a third of the nation's produce, and any disruption in production can lead to higher prices for consumers. According to a recent article, prices for fresh produce such as berries, broccoli, grapes, melons, and packaged salads have already increased due to the drought. Economists predict that these prices will continue to rise this year, with fresh fruit prices expected to increase by 2.5-3.5% and vegetable prices by 2-3%. The drought is also affecting the cattle industry in Texas and Oklahoma, leading to higher beef and veal prices. Additionally, tourists visiting California may notice changes in their hotel stays, such as requests to conserve water and linen and towel usage. While the drought has led to some negative consequences, it has also had a positive impact on California's wine industry, with record high wine grape harvests in recent years.\",\"source_segments\":[{\"index\":0,\"byte_start\":0,\"byte_end\":67},{\"index\":1,\"byte_start\":67,\"byte_end\":133},{\"index\":2,\"byte_start\":133,\"byte_end\":281},{\"index\":3,\"byte_start\":281,\"byte_end\":443},{\"index\":4,\"byte_start\":443,\"byte_end\":527},{\"index\":5,\"byte_start\":527,\"byte_end\":636},{\"index\":6,\"byte_start\":636,\"byte_end\":762},{\"index\":7,\"byte_start\":762,\"byte_end\":824},{\"index\":8,\"byte_start\":824,\"byte_end\":892},{\"index\":9,\"byte_start\":892,\"byte_end\":937},{\"index\":10,\"byte_start\":937,\"byte_end\":978},{\"index\":11,\"byte_start\":978,\"byte_end\":1019},{\"index\":12,\"byte_start\":1019,\"byte_end\":1066},{\"index\":13,\"byte_start\":1066,\"byte_end\":1108},{\"index\":14,\"byte_start\":1108,\"byte_end\":1293},{\"index\":15,\"byte_start\":1293,\"byte_end\":1442},{\"index\":16,\"byte_start\":1442,\"byte_end\":1552},{\"index\":17,\"byte_start\":1552,\"byte_end\":1727},{\"index\":18,\"byte_start\":1727,\"byte_end\":1851},{\"index\":19,\"byte_start\":1851,\"byte_end\":1948},{\"index\":20,\"byte_start\":1948,\"byte_end\":2042}